#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277040 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c6056ee-15f3-5b5c-8a14-173ea1da1381" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dd25a02-4e2e-592b-b869-2889bf56d0f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$H$ and $K$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277042} {:answer/id #uuid "a7780f8d-92ee-5bee-acda-ac38b221ba98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$J$ and $K$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277043} {:answer/id #uuid "d0033e22-6e20-53c9-b6ad-5502fdbe9286" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$H$ and $J$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277044} {:answer/id #uuid "66eb4339-b5c8-5b6f-a9c6-736fc2c5a548" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$H$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277045} {:answer/id #uuid "bc62ee22-7c25-5746-b95d-31e50a024745" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$J$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0033e22-6e20-53c9-b6ad-5502fdbe9286" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$H$ and $J$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277044} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e00ec5e-d2cb-5618-b9fc-572db656a6be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b4d40e2-069c-5e10-923d-ef1e82d45a9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-11717" :question/problem "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n\n$$\nH=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,K=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- For the matrix $H$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(H)=5\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 0=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(H)=0,$ we conclude that $H$ is not invertible. Since $H$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $H$ is singular.\n\n- For the matrix $J$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(J)=2\\cdot 6-3\\cdot 4=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(J)=0,$ we conclude that $J$ is not invertible. Since $J$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $J$ is singular.\n\n- The matrix $K$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix. So, it is not invertible. Since the matrix is not square, we can't say that it's singular.\n\nTherefore, only $H$ and $J$ is singular."}]}
